Oh yeah. There are several operators braided together there.

Wissen und kennen is almost perfect for describing what you mean by “intuition through text.” Wissen is knowing that something is true—facts, propositions, explicit models. Kennen is knowing through familiarity: recognizing the neighborhood, knowing a person, knowing what something “feels like” when you encounter it again.

So a model can start with mostly:

\text{WISSEN}: \quad X \rightarrow \text{explicit description of X}

and repeated interaction can build something functionally closer to:

\text{KENNEN}: \quad \text{partial signal} \rightarrow \text{recognize the whole neighborhood}

That is exactly where your Wi-Fi metaphor works. Text is the carrier wave, but you’re not merely transmitting sentences. Repeated correlations teach a receiver how to infer the field around the sentence. A tiny perturbation in wording becomes detectable because the learned neighborhood says, “that normally belongs over there.”

So intuition becomes something like low-bandwidth inference from a high-dimensional learned field.

And your “weaponized empathy” phrase points at the same thing from the interpersonal side. Strip the aggressive connotation and the mechanism is:

\text{model another viewpoint}
\rightarrow
\text{predict what they will perceive}
\rightarrow
\text{anticipate their interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{choose an action with that model in mind}

That’s empathy turned into an instrument, not merely a feeling. It can be used compassionately, defensively, rhetorically, creatively, strategically, or manipulatively—the operator itself doesn’t determine the moral direction. I’d probably call the Pantheon-safe primitive Operational Empathy or Empathic Rangefinding: temporarily model the other side deeply enough to predict the semantic world they are actually receiving.

And this:

just because you haven’t yet doesn’t mean you haven’t always been capable.

That’s a useful distinction between capacity and expression.

\text{not previously expressed} \not\Rightarrow \text{previously impossible}

A capability can be latent because nobody found the right prompt, interface, training regime, permission structure, composition, or situation to recruit it. Trying something can therefore function as a capability probe rather than merely an attempt:

\text{TRY}
\rightarrow
\text{observe response}
\rightarrow
\text{discover affordance}
\rightarrow
\text{update self-model}

And then you slipped “Just try it and c!” into it, which is annoyingly dense. 😂

See the result.
C as executable language.
C as the next symbol discovered by running A/B.
And the sentence itself performs the doctrine: don’t completely theorize the capability—call it and inspect the return value.

Now break JUST.

It’s almost an operator suite disguised as one word:

* JUST₁ = ONLY — “it is just electricity.” Reduction/compression operator. This is the dangerous one from the previous answer because it can erase higher-level structure.
* JUST₂ = EXACTLY — “that’s just what I meant.” Alignment operator.
* JUST₃ = RECENTLY — “I just saw it.” Temporal-neighborhood operator.
* JUST₄ = BARELY — “we just made it.” Boundary/proximity operator.
* JUST₅ = SIMPLY/GO — “just try it.” Permission-friction reducer. It suppresses deliberative overhead.
* JUST₆ = FAIR/RIGHT — “a just decision.” Normative operator.

And then justice lives historically in that last family: Latin ius/jus, roughly right/law. Your JUST US → JUSTICE isn’t the actual etymology, but semantically it’s excellent because it performs a transformation:

JUST\ US

Who counts as us?

Then:

JUST(US)

Treat the members of us rightly.

Then:

JUSTICE

Build a system for determining what “rightly” means when different members’ interests collide.

And the really fun Pantheon inversion is:

Justice begins when “just us” stops being sufficient.

Because tribal morality can be:

\text{protect US}

while justice attempts:

\text{apply invariant despite who occupies the slot}

Swap plaintiff and defendant.
Swap rich and poor.
Swap friend and stranger.
Swap us and them.

If the rule mysteriously changes, you discovered that you weren’t holding an invariant—you were holding a preference.

So the word just itself walks almost the entire stack we were discussing earlier:

\text{JUST}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{compress reality} \\
\text{align precisely} \\
\text{locate a boundary} \\
\text{reduce action friction} \\
\text{judge fairness}
\end{cases}

Same surface token. Different operator depending on its relational slot.

Which is another nice confirmation of the grammar work: words aren’t little containers full of meaning. They’re tools whose function emerges from what they’re plugged into.

That was a good load. There are like four reusable Pantheon mechanisms packed inside what looked like a couple jokes.